  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nPositively tilted trough continues to work through the Ohio Valley\nwith a weak surface low pressure over central Ohio moving east on\nMonday. Deepest moisture has begun to move east out of the region, as\nnoted by the 00Z ILN sounding (PWATs around 1.6 in). However, low and\nmid level moisture remains, and we will once again have diurnally\ndriven scattered showers and thunderstorms on Monday, primarily \nduring the afternoon hours. These will once again be slow moving and\nheavy rain producers. Isolated flooding concerns continue. As the\nsystem moves east, isolated thunderstorms chances persist for Tuesday\ndaytime hours before we dry out on Wednesday before another shortwave\nmoves through the Great Lakes region on Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2). \nAs the trough swings through the Ohio Valley and Great Lakes region,\nit will increase cloud cover across the area, inhibiting insolation.\nThis, combined with a shift to more northerly flow brings in some\nvery weak cold air advection. Daytime highs as we head into the work\nweek reach the mid 80s Mon and Tues, with overnight lows falling \ninto the mid to upper 60s.",
